<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chronicon terrae Prussiae</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1326">1326</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Chronicle of the Prussian Land</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chronicon terrae Prussiae, or Chronicle of the Prussian Land, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> completed in 1326 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-of-dusburg">Peter of Dusburg</a>, a priest of the Order. It tells of the Order's origins and its conquest of the pagan Prussians and wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> up to 1326. It is the main narrative source for the Prussian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, though written to celebrate the Order.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chronicon-terrae-prussiae.jpg" alt="Chronicon terrae Prussiae" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: pl:Piotr z Dusburga · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piotr_Dusburg_chronicle.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The chronicle was completed some 100 years after the crusaders first came into the Baltic region, and it is the first major chronicle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. It is divided into four volumes. The first gives the background of the Order and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusades</a> in Outremer, the second tells how the Order came to the Prussian land, and the third describes its wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Old Prussians</a> and other Baltic tribes. The fourth sets these events beside other events of the time elsewhere in the world.</p><p>An addendum of another 20 chapters covers the years 1326 to 1330 and may also be the work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-of-dusburg">Peter of Dusburg</a>. Peter drew on local monastery annals, chronicles, reports and oral accounts that he considered reliable, used the archive of the Grand Masters at Marienburg, and witnessed some of the events himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The work is written to glorify the Order's mission. Many chapters take the form of religious visions, miracles and lives of saints; the war against the pagans is presented as sacred, and every knight who falls in it goes to heaven. As a priest, Peter also wrote to teach his readers: the pagan Prussians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> are shown as pious in their own way, an example that should shame sinful and disobedient Christians.</p><p>The chronicle preserves some ethnographic information about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Old Prussians</a>, such as their sacred forests, fields and waters, where, as the Descriptiones terrarum of 1253 also records, no one was allowed to cut trees, plough or fish. It describes minor raids and clashes in great detail but takes no interest in the Order's domestic policy, saying nothing of its towns, trade or colonisation. Historians regard its accounts of events and battles as mostly reliable, while its chronology is at times wrong and its ethnographic material is ideologically charged.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chronicon terrae Prussiae” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Order produced a historical literature of its own, notably Peter of Dusburg's Latin <b>Chronicle of the Prussian Land</b>, completed in 1326 and put into German verse by Nicolaus von Jeroschin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
